{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "173",
    "case_summary": "A 32-year-old woman presented for evaluation of her worsening skin condition. The condition had progressively worsened to involve 90% of her body surface area (BSA).\n\nPatient had widespread polycyclic serpiginous, erythematous plaques with a double-edged scale on the trunk and extremities, involving 30% of the body surface area (BSA). Eyebrow hairs were sparse and less than 5 mm long, and her scalp hairs were coarse and brittle.\n\n- Skin biopsy: Showed psoriasiform epidermal hyperplasia with spongiosis\n- Trichography: Showed nodal swelling, with invagination of the distal hair into the proximal end\n- Clinical Images:\n  Image Title: Clinical Photographs Before and After Treatment\n  Image Description: Patient's shoulder showing skin condition\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Atopic dermatitis\nB. Pityriasis rubra pilaris\nC. Netherton syndrome\nD. Ichthyosis linearis circumflexa\n",
